    "response_version": 2,
    "assistant_text": "I’ve set up a comparison model around **productivity**. It includes your two options plus two reference cases: maintain current staffing and hire one developer.\n\nThe model currently has six unknown starting factors, and **maintain current staffing currently changes nothing**, so it cannot yet be compared. I can use the following **assumptions to test — not measurements**:\n\n**Current starting point**\n- Developer hires: **0 hires**\n- Tech lead hires: **0 hires**\n- Engineering capacity: **50 / 100** — neutral placeholder; current baseline unknown\n- Coordination quality: **50 / 100** — neutral placeholder; current state unknown\n- Technical direction quality: **50 / 100** — neutral placeholder; current state unknown\n- Onboarding demand: **0 additional hires being onboarded**\n\n**What each option sets**\n- Hire Tech Lead: **1 Tech Lead hire**\n- Hire Two Developers: **2 developer hires**\n- Hire One Developer: **1 developer hire**\n- Maintain Current Staffing: **0 developer hires; 0 Tech Lead hires**\n\nThe model left out “zero developers” for the Tech Lead option (and equivalent non-actions for the developer options) because those options are not connected to those factors; that is appropriate, not an assumed effect.\n\nThe key real-world questions are: your existing team size, whether the Tech Lead would still deliver directly or chiefly lead, and your onboarding/mentoring capacity.\n\nWould you like to adopt these starting assumptions so we can examine what the model implies?\n\nThe model was saved. Questions this model does not answer yet: What is the current team size and current productivity baseline? How much delivery work would a new Tech Lead perform directly versus spend on leadership and coordination? What is the expected onboarding time and available mentoring capacity for additional developers?",
